import React, { useState, useEffect, useMemo, useRef } from 'react';

/**
 * ============================================================================
 * PRODSCHEDULE: PRODUCTION SCHEDULE HUB
 * ============================================================================
 * A copy of the Manager hub trimmed to the production schedule only, plus:
 *   1. IMPORT WEEKS  - bring in the weekly Excel plans: many files, every week sheet in each, or a pasted table. A year of
 *                      weeks can go in at once. Each line has a pattern (Breading = night shift 6 PM to 4 AM, IBL 4 =
 *                      non-stop, WJ and IBL 5 = day run cleaned at night) that sets where its runs start and what a blank
 *                      hours cell is filled to. The same week in two sheets: the later sheet wins. Re-importing a week
 *                      UPDATES the runs it already made (found by line, item and start, however the area was spelled),
 *                      so a second upload shows ALREADY THERE and changes nothing.
 *                      NOTES in the sheet are saved with the run: free text under a day, an Excel cell note, or a Notes
 *                      column. A note under a day goes to that day's run; one on the item goes to all its runs; notes that
 *                      find no run are listed, never dropped. Each upload is tagged ("2026-10-09 08:30 · Week 41") so a bad
 *                      one can be found. After Apply the weeks are read back and compared with what was sent.
 *                      Steps 1, 2 and 3 (and each week and line in step 3) fold away; the Apply bar stays pinned at the
 *                      bottom whichever step is folded, and Stop ends a long upload (carry on with Apply again).
 *   2. SCHEDULE      - Gantt over 1 day to 8 weeks. Drag a run to move it (or to another line), drag its edges to
 *                      change start / stop, drag it past a neighbour to swap the order, double-click to edit,
 *                      double-click an empty spot to add a run. Undo keeps the last 30 changes.
 *                      Every MSS production line (areas flagged "runs product") has its row, even before it has a run.
 *                      The chart has day lines (Sundays stronger), hour lines (every hour on a day, every 6 h over a week,
 *                      noon over 2 weeks), weekend and night shading and a NOW tag, like the Ganttly; its header stays
 *                      at the top while the lines scroll. Each line is drawn on its own, so a click or drag only redraws
 *                      the line it touches.
 *   LINE PICKER      - The Run dialog and every Import line card pick the line from the MSS list (search, arrow keys,
 *                      Enter). "IBL 4", "ibl4" and "IBL4" are the same line; "IBL 4" with only IBL in the list is IBL line 4.
 *                      A name not in the list is still allowed and is saved as typed.
 *   3. MONTH         - month calendar, drag a run to another day, double-click to edit.
 *   4. SHIFTS+ROSTER - production and sanitation shifts (headcount, start / stop) per line, shown as an overlay
 *                      on the Gantt, with the roster read from the employee table.
 *   5. CLEANING      - equipment cleaning time (craa5_aa_assets) per line. Every gap between two runs is checked:
 *                      is there enough time (and enough sanitation people) to clean?
 *   6. DATA CHECK    - Run check: reads the runs of a period a few weeks at a time and lists the ones that are NOT SAVED
 *                      COMPLETELY (no start or stop, backwards, no line number or product) or are NOT ON AN MSS PRODUCTION
 *                      AREA (blank, spelled differently, "IBL 4" against IBL4, not an MSS area, not a production line),
 *                      plus duplicates and overlaps. Area problems are grouped ("IBL 4" on 214 runs is one card with one
 *                      button); every fix can be undone. Nothing changes until a fix is pressed.
 *                      Columns: every Dataverse column the app reads or writes, what it is for, and found / missing.
 *                      Payloads: every save of this visit with the exact columns sent and any column Dataverse refused.
 *                      The header pill always shows the last save.
 *
 *   7. CHANGE REQUESTS - the "Schedule Change Request Form" Supply Chain sends when the schedule moves inside the time fence. Upload
 *                      the Excel file (or paste its cells) and every line is matched to a run by item number and original date:
 *                      a quantity change shortens or lengthens the run in proportion to its cases (12 h at 9,900 cases is 6 h at
 *                      4,950; hours can be typed), a date change moves it, a product TAKEN OFF removes it, a product ADDED takes its
 *                      line, start time and hours per case from its last runs. Later runs are pushed when the change overlaps them.
 *                      Nothing is saved until Apply; a line that is not certain (several runs fit, the cases disagree, the run has
 *                      started or over, the form puts a change in the fixed zone with no approval) is not ticked and says why. Every
 *                      changed run (not the ones only pushed later) gets a note and a batch tag; the schedule is read back after the
 *                      save and Undo puts it all back, also after a reload (the last applies are kept in this browser for 14 days,
 *                      newest first). The same form read twice changes nothing twice. "Applies by itself when sure" (off until switched
 *                      on) applies a request without a click when every line is certain. An email flow (Power Automate) can drop forms
 *                      in the optional table craa5_schedulechange; the tab reads the waiting ones every 2 minutes while the page is open,
 *                      claims a row (status "applying") before it applies it by itself, and writes applied / review / failed back to
 *                      the row. Rows left in review or failed come back on the next visit and wait for a person.
 *
 *   8. CLEAN PLAN    - what every line needs cleaned, worked out from the runs with ONE set of rules (the CLEANING RULES block,
 *                      also behind the Gantt, the Checks, the Run dialog and the previews): an allergen clean after an allergen
 *                      run, each line's daily (full) clean in its longest stop of the day, product changes, the crew on shift
 *                      walked through the stop, and the days the MSS needs a Daily clean (the MSS line rules, copied exactly).
 *                      Directors get the hangups first (short, no crew, overlaps). It is published to the optional table
 *                      craa5_cleanplan for the MSS calendar, the Ganttly / PBED, Shift Start and the Manager hub to read:
 *                      a few seconds after any run change on this page ("Publish by itself", last week to 8 weeks ahead,
 *                      only rows that changed) or by hand. The cleaning and shift settings it uses are shared in that table
 *                      ("Share these settings"), so every page that publishes gives the same answer. The reader guide is in
 *                      the project files (production-schedule/clean-plan).
 *
 * SCROLLING: the host canvas never scrolls the page, so the app is one screen tall (h-screen) and the area under the
 * header scrolls on its own (<main>), the same shell as the Manager hub.
 *
 * DATAVERSE MAPPING
 * | craa5__aa_prd_schd   | Runs (source of truth). Same columns the Manager hub uses.                    |
 * | craa5_aa_mss_area    | MSS areas = the lines. Areas flagged craa5_runsproduct = yes are offered (and get   |
 * |                      | a row on the Gantt); older copies of the table (craa5__aa_areas) work as a fallback. |
 * | craa5_aa_assets      | Equipment + cleaning minutes (craa5_hoursrequired = base minutes,              |
 * |                      | craa5_MINALRG = allergen minutes, craa5_heads, craa5_areaname).                |
 * | craa5__aa_employee   | Roster (name, craa5_shift = crew A-D, role, job-role lookup).                  |
 * | craa5_schedulechange | OPTIONAL inbox for change request forms: craa5_name, craa5_formjson (the form's cells as JSON), |
 * |                      | craa5_status (new / applied / review / failed / dismissed), craa5_result, craa5_sender. |
 * | craa5_cleanplan      | OPTIONAL Clean Plan written by this page for the other apps (columns: Data Check > Columns). |
 *
 * OPTIONAL COLUMNS on craa5__aa_prd_schd (add them so nothing stays only in the browser; Data Check > Columns lists them):
 *     craa5_ordernumber (Single line of text)    craa5_cases (Whole number)    craa5_notes (Multiple lines of text, 2000)
 *     craa5_mssareaguid (Single line of text, 36: the id of the run's MSS area)    craa5_importbatch (Single line of text, 200)
 * Until they exist the app still works: Dataverse names a missing column when a save is refused, the app remembers that for
 * the visit, saves the rest, keeps Order # / Cases / Notes on this browser only, and says so (banner, header pill, payload list).
 * "I added them, check again" (or Reload) tries them again.
 *
 * BROWSER STORAGE: line mapping, start times, shifts, cleaning settings and roster overrides are kept in this
 * browser (localStorage), like the Ganttly. Runs themselves always live in Dataverse. The cleaning and shift settings can also
 * be shared through the Clean Plan table (Clean Plan tab); a browser with none of its own takes the shared ones.
 * ============================================================================
 */

